    {
      "id": "b-2",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "I’ll scan the repo and open the editor page to assess current UX and code structure."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-3",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "I think it's important to add keyboard navigation to move selected elements with the arrow keys, along with handlers for zooming in and out using Cmd/+ and - or bracket keys. I should also include “Fit to screen” and “Actual size” controls since we already have reset options. Adding ARIA live regions for zoom percentages and providing aria-label for the canvas container is crucial too. It makes sense to add tabIndex={0} for keyboard handling and to ensure there are screen-reader-only instructions!"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-4",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "I want to provide a toggle and additional helpful overlays using CSS will-change: transform for smoother performance. I could convert the custom wheel event into a passive: false listener. It’s important to ensure buttons have accessible labels; they included titles for reset and recenter, but the zoom buttons need aria-label and title. Adding tooltips might also be beneficial. I’ll implement keyboard controls for zooming and panning, and consider using role=\"application\" cautiously for full-screen interactions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-5",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For now, I can skip adding role=\"region\" and aria-roledescription=\"canvas editor\", but I should provide aria-keyshortcuts to describe key functions. Adding CSS for a focus ring on the canvas container is essential, and I also want to create an aria-live=\"polite\" region for zoom status updates. The approach here should involve minimal code modifications while enhancing accessibility. I’ll propose a plan to implement these improvements, including adding accessible labels, keyboard controls, and some performance tweaks."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-6",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "I want to add tabIndex={0} to the canvasRef container to make it focusable for keyboard handling. For the keyboard controls, I was thinking about replacing global keydown/keyup events for space with events specifically for the window. However, to allow arrow key navigation while keeping the focus on the editor container, I'll need to check if the canvas contains document.activeElement or if a store variable \"isEditorActive\" is true. Instead of using a complex pointer approach, I should unify the event handling to the container to streamline the process and prevent interference with other page sections."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-7",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "I think it's important to add keyboard navigation to move selected elements with the arrow keys, along with handlers for zooming in and out using Cmd/+ and - or bracket keys. I should also include “Fit to screen” and “Actual size” controls since we already have reset options. Adding ARIA live regions for zoom percentages and providing aria-label for the canvas container is crucial too. It makes sense to add tabIndex={0} for keyboard handling and to ensure there are screen-reader-only instructions!"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-8",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "I want to provide a toggle and additional helpful overlays using CSS will-change: transform for smoother performance. I could convert the custom wheel event into a passive: false listener. It’s important to ensure buttons have accessible labels; they included titles for reset and recenter, but the zoom buttons need aria-label and title. Adding tooltips might also be beneficial. I’ll implement keyboard controls for zooming and panning, and consider using role=\"application\" cautiously for full-screen interactions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-9",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For now, I can skip adding role=\"region\" and aria-roledescription=\"canvas editor\", but I should provide aria-keyshortcuts to describe key functions. Adding CSS for a focus ring on the canvas container is essential, and I also want to create an aria-live=\"polite\" region for zoom status updates. The approach here should involve minimal code modifications while enhancing accessibility. I’ll propose a plan to implement these improvements, including adding accessible labels, keyboard controls, and some performance tweaks."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-10",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "I want to add tabIndex={0} to the canvasRef container to make it focusable for keyboard handling. For the keyboard controls, I was thinking about replacing global keydown/keyup events for space with events specifically for the window. However, to allow arrow key navigation while keeping the focus on the editor container, I'll need to check if the canvas contains document.activeElement or if a store variable \"isEditorActive\" is true. Instead of using a complex pointer approach, I should unify the event handling to the container to streamline the process and prevent interference with other page sections."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-11",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "I can keep the window event for the space key while adding onKeyDown and onKeyUp for arrow keys and zoom on the container. For arrow keys to work, I’ll set a tabIndex={0} to focus on the canvas so users can click and use keys. The onKeyDown will handle zoom in with = or +, zoom out with -, reset with 0, and recenter with f. For moving selected elements with arrow keys, I'll implement it simply. Lastly, I’ll add the necessary ARIA attributes for accessibility and ensure the usability of these controls."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-12",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Editedpage.tsx"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-13",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "I used the zoom variable to compute srStatus for the new zoom percentage by multiplying zoom by 1.2. Since setZoom happens within handlers next, it complicates using the old zoom for srStatus. When zoom is near its limits, it will clamp in handleZoomIn/out. While I initially considered maintaining the handleZoomIn/out approach, I realized updating srStatus with useEffect when zoom changes is cleaner. This way, we avoid redundant updates during clicks."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-14",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "I used the zoom variable to compute srStatus for the new zoom percentage by multiplying zoom by 1.2. Since setZoom happens within handlers next, it complicates using the old zoom for srStatus. When zoom is near its limits, it will clamp in handleZoomIn/out. While I initially considered maintaining the handleZoomIn/out approach, I realized updating srStatus with useEffect when zoom changes is cleaner. This way, we avoid redundant updates during clicks."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-15",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "I’m planning to implement useEffect for handling zoom changes and simplify the code by removing the modifications in the onClick function. This change will make it accessible for screen readers. I’ll remove the setSrStatus calls from onClick and integrate the useEffect hook instead. I've added aria-describedby for accessibility and ensured the container has tabIndex={0}. I also want to include onKeyDown, but onKeyUp isn't necessary right now, so let's proceed with the update."
        }
      ]
    },
